(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110300-kotoba-adl-convert-asks-whether-a-clojure-consumer-remains")
  (:adr/topic "kotoba-adl-convert-set-aware-guards")
  (:adr/title "kotoba adl convert asks whether a Clojure consumer remains, not whether one exists")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110200-a-moving-defcid-reaches-the-conformance-corpus-and-no-deployment-lock"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

Owner instruction 2026-09-10: improve the performance of kotoba convert.

Measured first, because 'performance' had two candidate meanings and only one
of them was the problem. Throughput was never the bottleneck -- a whole
repository classifies in seconds. The bottleneck was the SUCCESS RATE:
`kotoba adl convert` converted nothing at all.

    for each of eight real target repositories:
      cd <repo> && kbb --backend sci --classpath <kotoba>/src <kotoba>/bin/kotoba_adl.cljs \\
        convert <repo>/src

    SCANNED 56    WOULD-CONVERT 0    REFUSED 56 compiled-by-build-config

## What was actually wrong

Two guards decide whether a source file may be renamed, and both asked the
wrong question. They asked 'does a consumer exist?' when the question they
exist to answer is 'will a Clojure consumer REMAIN after this pass?'.

Converting a tree file-by-file, every file blocks every other: A requires B so
B is refused, and A is refused for its own requirer. A tree that is entirely
self-consistent converts nothing.

## What was deliberately NOT done

The build guard was added the same day and it is the reason the number is zero.
Removing it would have raised the number immediately, and it would have been
the exact failure this workspace names -- shrinking a predicate to make a gate
green.

Its rationale is a property, not an implementation state, and it names a real
incident: nbb and the clojure CLI resolve a namespace from `:paths` by
EXTENSION, so renaming a file to `.kotoba` makes it invisible to the build and
nothing reports an error. kotoba-lang/datalog was renamed while three nbb
scripts still required `datalog.core`, and the datom query face has been dead
since. A rename does not fail.

Measured at the commit before that guard, on the same corpus: 7 of 56
convertible. Those seven were not a capability that was lost -- they were seven
files that would have gone silently out of their build.

## The change

The set of paths converting in a pass is threaded through classification.

- A consumer that is ITSELF converting in this pass is not a Clojure consumer
  afterwards, so it is not evidence against converting the file.
- A source root that converts ENTIRELY leaves no Clojure namespace under it for
  any runtime to resolve by extension. A root that converts PARTIALLY is still
  refused, and that partial case is exactly what the datalog incident was.

Whether one file may convert depends on which others convert, so the answer is
a fixpoint, not a single pass: start optimistic, classify, drop what was
refused, repeat. The set only shrinks, so it terminates. The iteration bound is
REPORTED, and a run that does not settle exits 2 rather than reporting a pass.

## Result, isolated

The comparison holds the input fixed -- whole repository, two repositories, 65
files -- and varies only the converter, so the improvement is not confounded
with the change in how it is invoked:

    unmodified           11 convertible, 41 refused compiled-by-build-config
    set-aware fixpoint   17 convertible, 35 refused compiled-by-build-config

Plus six files, and the guard still refuses thirty-five.

## The control, which is the half that matters

A change that only makes a number go up is indistinguishable from a change that
disabled a check. So the same improved code was handed a PARTIAL set -- `src/`
only, while `test/` and `scripts/` keep requiring those namespaces:

    SCANNED 21    WOULD-CONVERT 0    REFUSED 21 compiled-by-build-config

Same code, two inputs, opposite verdicts. The guard fires when it should and
stands down when it should not.

The evidence floor was re-checked in the same pass and still refuses: an empty
scan prints `Refusing to report a pass: scanned 0 files` and exits 2. That exit
code had to be taken from a file rather than a pipe -- read through `| tail` it
reads 0, which is `tail`'s status and not the check's.

## What this does NOT claim

Converting is still not compiling. A converted file is renamed, not translated:
measured the same day, 30 landed `.kotoba` files drawn from 30 distinct
repositories were put through `amu check` and 0 passed, while the control -- a
real Kotoba module -- passed. The rejection reasons are the work list, and two
classes are two thirds of it: `:kotoba/source-read-failed` and
`:kotoba.error/map-literal-value`, 11 each of 30.

So this ADR moves one number: how much of a repository `kotoba adl convert` is
willing to touch. It does not move the number that matters for Q9, which is how
much of it the compiler accepts.")))
